### What is the left view of a binary tree?

The left view is what you would see standing on the left side of the tree: one node per level, the leftmost. It is the mirror of the right view. For each level, only the first node processed in a BFS is visible.

```java
package com.bank.model;

public class RankNode {
    public String name;
    public RankNode left;
    public RankNode right;

    public RankNode(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
}
```

### How do you compute the left view with BFS?

The same BFS level-size snapshot as the right view, but capture the node at index 0 instead of levelSize - 1.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.RankNode;
import java.util.*;

public class LeftViewBFS {
    public static List<String> leftView(RankNode root) {
        List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();
        if (root == null) return result;

        Queue<RankNode> queue = new LinkedList<>();
        queue.offer(root);

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            int levelSize = queue.size();
            for (int i = 0; i < levelSize; i++) {
                RankNode node = queue.poll();
                if (i == 0) {
                    result.add(node.name); // first of this level
                }
                if (node.left != null) queue.offer(node.left);
                if (node.right != null) queue.offer(node.right);
            }
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        //       CEO
        //      /   \
        //    VP1   VP2
        //           \
        //           DIR2
        RankNode ceo = new RankNode("CEO");
        ceo.left = new RankNode("VP1");
        ceo.right = new RankNode("VP2");
        ceo.right.right = new RankNode("DIR2");

        System.out.println("Left view: " + leftView(ceo));
    }
}
```

```text
Left view: [CEO, VP1, DIR2]
```

### Why is the left view not just the left spine?

If the right subtree runs deeper than the left, the first node on a deeper level comes from the right side. Following only left children misses it.

```java
// WRONG: following left children only
public static List<String> naiveLeftView(RankNode root) {
    List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();
    while (root != null) {
        result.add(root.name);
        root = root.left; // misses right-side deeper nodes!
    }
    return result;
}
// Returns [CEO, VP1] but misses DIR2 at level 2
```

### How does the DFS alternative work?

Visit left child before right, carrying depth. Record a node when its depth equals the current result size, meaning it is the first node seen at that depth.

```java
public static List<String> leftViewDFS(RankNode root) {
    List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();
    dfs(root, 0, result);
    return result;
}

private static void dfs(RankNode node, int depth, List<String> result) {
    if (node == null) return;
    if (depth == result.size()) result.add(node.name);
    dfs(node.left, depth + 1, result);  // left first for left view
    dfs(node.right, depth + 1, result);
}
```

### How are left and right views related?

They are mirrors of the same BFS pattern. The only difference is the capture condition:

| View | Capture condition |
|---|---|
| Right view | `i == levelSize - 1` (last of level) |
| Left view | `i == 0` (first of level) |

```java
// Both views share this skeleton
while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
    int levelSize = queue.size();
    for (int i = 0; i < levelSize; i++) {
        RankNode node = queue.poll();
        // Right view: if (i == levelSize - 1) result.add(node);
        // Left view:  if (i == 0) result.add(node);
        if (node.left != null) queue.offer(node.left);
        if (node.right != null) queue.offer(node.right);
    }
}
```

### What are the edge cases?

Empty tree, single node, and a tree where every level has only right children.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.RankNode;

public class LeftViewEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Empty: " + LeftViewBFS.leftView(null));
        System.out.println("Single: " + LeftViewBFS.leftView(new RankNode("CEO")));

        RankNode rightOnly = new RankNode("CEO");
        rightOnly.right = new RankNode("VP");
        rightOnly.right.right = new RankNode("DIR");
        System.out.println("Right-only: " + LeftViewBFS.leftView(rightOnly));
    }
}
```

```text
Empty: []
Single: [CEO]
Right-only: [CEO, VP, DIR]
```

### What is the complexity?

BFS visits each node once. Time is O(n), space is O(w) for the queue. DFS uses O(h) recursion stack space.

### What should a senior engineer know?

Presenting the left and right views together as the symmetric first-node and last-node rules within the same BFS pattern shows the interviewer you see the whole Tree BFS family as one coherent pattern.

In production at National Bank of India, the left view produces an entry-point summary showing the first approver reachable at each escalation rank.

```java
// Left view for entry-point summary
List<String> entryApprovers = LeftViewBFS.leftView(approvalTree);
for (int rank = 0; rank < entryApprovers.size(); rank++) {
    System.out.println("Rank " + rank + " entry: " + entryApprovers.get(rank));
}
```
